; `commit` only ever closes a `sandbox` block -- every other block type already has its own
; way to keep what happened inside (dedenting, or `qed`/`thus`, running the actual inference
; rule for that block kind), so `commit` on any of them is rejected rather than silently
; doing something else.
;
; note: this can't use `expect` (see forall-intro-rejects-leaked-constant.kurt for the same
; reasoning) -- the failure happens while `commit` itself tries to close the `proof` block,
; not from an ordinary statement inside it; hence the older `;;; ` marker convention here.
bool A
show A implies A
proof
    commit
;;; EvalError: `commit` only closes a `sandbox` block, not a `proof` block
